From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] winspool: a printer maker's driver package installs from its INF, and a CUPS printer prints through it

A maker's installer leaves its printer driver package (an INF of class
Printer with the driver's files) in the driver store, as DYMO Connect's does
with DYMO_LW5xx.inf; nothing installed it, and InstallPrinterDriverFromPackage
did not exist. It now does: the model's install section (decorated for the
platform), DriverFile/ConfigFile/DataFile/HelpFile, CopyFiles (single files
and file sections), the core driver from CoreDriverSections or Needs
(Unidrv: unidrv.dll and unidrvui.dll), the files found by SourceDisksNames
and SourceDisksFiles, added with AddPrinterDriverEx. A CUPS queue whose model
has such a package is given its Windows driver (installed if need be; a model
looked for in vain is looked for again only when the packages change), with
the winprint print processor; the PostScript driver made from the queue's
PPD no longer overwrites it, and the printer's port stays CUPS (CUPS keeps
the device; the driver's output goes to it raw, 0925). The printer's
"SgPrinterDriver" value "linux" chooses the PostScript driver instead, under
"<model> (CUPS)". A 32-bit program is answered for the 64-bit driver when
only that one is installed (as through Windows' splwow64), and loads our own
configuration DLL by name. A queue we publish for Linux programs to print
through a Windows driver ("... (maker's driver)") is not a Windows printer.

Drivers are the Windows system's to install, as on Windows (point and
print): only a process that may write the machine's driver keys and the
drivers' directory installs one -- an administrator, or SYSTEM's side
(splwow64 drivers, run by sg-printers-refresh and sg-windows-printers). A
standard user's InstallPrinterDriverFromPackage is refused at once
(E_ACCESSDENIED), and a standard user's program neither installs a maker's
package nor makes a new CUPS printer (a driver registered without its files,
or a printer without a driver, was left for every user); the printer is made
by the Windows system, and until it is the queue is looked at again. A
printer that could not be made (no temporary folder for its PPD yet, as
while a prefix is being built) is tried again by the next program, not
taken as done.

Test: test/printdrv-gate.sh (packages installed from INF files; mutant
SG_MUTANT_PKGINSTALL), test/cupsdrv-gate.sh with a CUPS server of its own
(mutants SG_MUTANT_WINDRIVER, SG_MUTANT_WINQUEUE) and
test/stduserprint-gate.sh, a second Unix user as the standard user of a
shared prefix and server (mutants SG_MUTANT_DRVACCESS,
SG_MUTANT_PRINTERSYSTEM).
